    {
      "number": 18,
      "id": "PR_kwDOQNz9Nc8AAAABA67SZQ",
      "title": "[cubic] specify W_max to be set",
      "url": "https://github.com/kazuho/draft-kazuho-ccwg-rapid-start/pull/18",
      "state": "OPEN",
      "author": "kazuho",
      "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "The obvious answer is to set W_max to the full BDP estimate measured by Rapid Start.\r\n\r\nHowever, when competing flows exist, adopting the full BDP estimate makes the flow less aggressive than flows that used slow start, which adopt an estimate twice as large when slow start fully overshot. To compensate for that, the PR recommends adopting 2x full BDP estimate - a value on par with slow start - as W_max, when competing flows exist and hence fast convergence is active.\r\n\r\nThe charts below are from an experiment on a simulator: https://github.com/h2o/quicly/tree/experiment/newcomer-startup\r\n\r\nThey show the share of bandwidth taken by the newcomer across time. Bold lines are the median, dotted lines are the 10th and 90th percentiles. All runs used Rapid Start's IW of 60. \r\n\r\nCompare the purple line (traditional slow start) and the blue line (Rapid Start setting W_max to full BDP estimate). As can be seen, on long, fat networks (5G, LTE), the negative impact of not growing early persists. Disabling the entry to fast convergence in the first CA recovery (the green line) sometimes moves the needle, but not always. Setting W_max to 2x the estimate (the red line) trails the slow start curve better across these simulator runs.\r\n\r\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"950\" alt=\"newcomer-startup-5g\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f6852490-09e0-409e-bac2-d81cf94976e1\" />\r\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"950\" alt=\"newcomer-startup-lte\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d6a3615a-d99c-4e0d-91c0-b577d1ddd712\" />\r\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"950\" alt=\"newcomer-startup-dsl\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4f8453f9-665d-4c4f-aa9a-d8a77d0c7e4d\" />\r\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"950\" alt=\"newcomer-startup-half-dsl\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/02846e0c-4aa1-402e-8f47-8ca193c8eddb\" />\r\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"950\" alt=\"newcomer-startup-quarter-dsl\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7f945365-4b49-4ac8-a600-6a8ee6755632\" />\r\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"950\" alt=\"newcomer-startup-eighth-dsl\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7aafe670-e479-4c09-9ac0-1925194c0081\" />\r\n\r\nThe downside of setting Wmax to 2x BDP is that, when there were no competing flows, the flow exits the first CA period at an earlier moment than when adopting 1x BDP. However, the first CA period still persists for a handful of round-trips, and that is much better than not doing the Rapid-Start-style reduction at all.\r\n\r\n<img width=\"1200\" height=\"1064\" alt=\"simulator-plot-20260825191344\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/063794be-c16c-4ff4-9570-20352f5ee464\" />\r\n<img width=\"1200\" height=\"1064\" alt=\"simulator-plot-20260825191335\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5dee40f0-d2f8-4d99-bcf7-573f7457fd45\" />\r\n",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-25T10:17:44Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-26T12:18:21Z",
      "baseRepository": "kazuho/draft-kazuho-ccwg-rapid-start",
      "baseRefName": "main",
      "baseRefOid": "1ceca99b56441f5d5ea226a21df1e2dfabadc308",
      "headRepository": "kazuho/draft-kazuho-ccwg-rapid-start",
      "headRefName": "kazuho/cubic-wmax",
      "headRefOid": "f414212a14a41cb5c958175a2143e2a2b5bb8a02",
      "closedAt": null,
      "mergedAt": null,
      "mergedBy": null,
      "mergeCommit": null,
      "comments": [
        {
          "author": "mwelzl",
          "authorAssociation": "NONE",
          "body": "Where does \"Rapid Start's IW of 60\" come from? The draft says the IW can be up to 2x the one of \"classic paced slow start\", i.e. 2*10 = 20, I guess?\r\n\r\nAlso, regarding the downside with only one flow, I think that comes from the value of beta in the first back-off. The \"ideal\" value in this corner case will depend on the length of the transmission, which isn't normally known to TCP and probably nothing we should try to optimize for. So, I think that's best ignored.",
          "createdAt": "2026-08-26T12:08:10Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-08-26T12:08:10Z"
        }
      ],
